(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2608211900-a-sparse-worktree-commit-deleted-the-repository-twice")
  (:adr/title "A session-close commit deleted 231,009 files and merged to main; the same shape had already happened the day before")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-21")
  (:adr/tags "git sparse-checkout main incident detector session-close")
  (:adr/related "adr-2608136000-five-questions adr-2608136800-checkout-west-pin-and-main-are-three-things adr-2608135200-reading-a-stale-checkout-as-authoritative")
  (:adr/body
  "## What happened

`298aab24d` -- titled `adr: session close -- maturity ingest wave + JVM exit tranche` --
added one ADR and deleted 231,009 files: every top-level entry of the superproject
except `90-docs`. It merged to main as `31fad05c`. For the duration, the tip of
`com-junkawasaki/root` carried a tree with a single directory in it. No `manifest/`,
no `scripts/`, no `orgs/`, no `.claude/`, no `CLAUDE.md`.

It was not noticed by the session that made it, by the merge, or by anything
downstream. I found it only because I was resolving an unrelated 45-commit
divergence and the Contents API answered 404 for `manifest/west.yml`. I first read
that 404 as drift and aborted; the abort was right for the wrong reason. The real
cause of the 404 turned out to be unrelated (west.yml is 1,052,663 bytes, over the
Contents API's 1 MB ceiling), and chasing it is what surfaced the empty tree.

## It is not a one-off

Scanning three days of main for commits deleting more than 500 files finds two:

| commit | date | deletions | top-level entries left | title |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `c40426acb` | 2026-08-20 | 232,718 | 4 | maturity: remeasure after usa-hhs axis-ingest landing |
| `298aab24d` | 2026-08-21 | 231,009 | 1 | adr: session close -- maturity ingest wave + JVM exit tranche |

Both are in main's history. The first was undone by a later merge whose other side
won -- accidentally, not by anyone noticing. Two occurrences in two days, from two
different loop-driven sessions, is a mechanism, not an accident.

## The mechanism

This is the sparse-checkout shape CLAUDE.md already describes in prose: files outside
the cone are absent from the working tree, and `git add -A` / `git commit -a` records
absent as deleted. The section that warns about it is about *reading* -- concluding
that something is missing because `ls` cannot see it. The same absence is also a
*writing* hazard, and nothing enforced that half.

Both commits are self-describing. `298aab24d` diffstats as 1 addition and 231,009
deletions. The number is in the commit itself. Nobody and nothing read it.

## Why the fix is forward-only

`256b6b8f` restores the tree by taking `36f309f3`'s tree (the last good one) and
adding back the single ADR that `298aab24d` legitimately meant to land, with the bad
merge as its parent. It was merged server-side as `df4575fb`. No history was
rewritten and nothing was force-pushed, per CLAUDE.md; the bad merge stays in the log
where it can be read. Main is back to 48 top-level entries, verified against the tree
API after landing -- `manifest/`, `scripts/`, `CLAUDE.md` all present.

Nothing was lost: the only main commits between the last good tree and the restore
were the bad merge itself and the restore.

## What is missing, and it is a detector

There is no check that reads a commit's own diffstat before it lands. This is the
class ADR-2608136000 names: the check that could not measure returns the same value
as the check that measured no problem -- except here there is no check at all, so
`git commit` returns 0 for `deleted the repository` exactly as it does for `added an
ADR`. Success and catastrophe are the same exit code.

The guard is cheap and belongs in two places:

1. A PreToolUse hook on `git commit` that refuses when the staged deletion count
   exceeds a threshold and the working tree is a sparse checkout
   (`git config core.sparseCheckout` is true), naming the count and the cone. Sparse
   is the discriminator -- a genuine mass deletion in a full checkout is rare but
   legitimate; a mass deletion in a sparse one is almost always this bug.
2. A fleet gate that scans main for commits whose deletion count exceeds a threshold
   and whose tree has fewer top-level entries than its parent. That one is the
   backstop, and it answers a question nobody has been able to ask: has this happened
   before? On three days of history the answer was twice.

Neither exists yet. This ADR is the record and the resume point, not the fix.

## Consequence for closing

Session-close is when this fires. Both commits were a session writing its record at
the end of its work, in the worktree it had been working in. The closing procedure in
CLAUDE.md says to update the authoritative record and land the PR; it does not say to
look at what the commit contains. Adding one line to that procedure -- read the
diffstat before landing a close commit -- would have caught both.")))
